  "body": "## Cause\n\n`band_reach` (`crates/sim/src/verify/reach.rs:72-80` on main) slices the model at each distinct end Z of a cutting move (`rest.islands_at`). It adds the boundary samples of each slice to ONE sample set. Then it tests each band disc of each move against the whole set (`reach.rs:180-193`). The cost is moves x planes x samples. A ramp entry ends each move at its own Z, so the cost grows with the square of the moves.\n\n## Fix\n\n- A new private index, `SampleGrid` (`crates/sim/src/verify/sample_grid.rs`), holds the samples. It keeps each sample one time for each exact bit pattern, and puts the samples in square XY cells.\n- Each band disc reads only the cells that its box touches. The box is wider than the disc by a relative 1e-9, against rounding.\n- A sample or a query that the grid cannot place (NaN, infinity, very far out, a box wider than 64 cells, a bad cell width) is read by all queries, or the query reads all samples.\n- The sample set stays ONE set for all planes. The sweep (c3) gets the deduplicated samples.\n- `band_reach` now calls `band_reach_counted`, which returns `ReachStats` (slices asked, sample tests). Only the tests read it.\n\n## Why the findings are identical\n\n- The law reads no plane of a sample: a sample from a plane far below a move can convict it. So this PR does NOT split the planes.\n- Each reading of a sample (`in_stock`, `surface`, `part_at`) is pure in the sample. The verdict of a disc is a max (part, then stock, then clean). The order and a repeated sample cannot change it.\n- The grid gives a superset of the samples that the old distance skip kept. Where a NaN makes the old skip fail, the grid reads the sample too.\n- The sweep (c3) uses a max and a memo keyed by the exact bits (`sweep.rs`), so the deduplicated set gives the same answer.\n- The old function stays as a test-only reference, `crates/sim/src/verify/reach_reference.rs` (`#[cfg(test)]`, line for line, plus two counters). `verify::reach_tests` compares the warnings, in order, and the slice count.\n\nEquivalence cases (`indexed_band_reach_keeps_every_finding_of_the_pooled_scan`, `degenerate_paths_match_the_pooled_scan_and_do_not_panic`, and the count pin):\n1. planar rings round a boss, clear (Area and Boundary mode);\n2. planar rings 1 mm off the boss walls: real `ShankHit { part: true }` findings;\n3. a helix of 216 chords (many planes);\n4. many-plane ramps of 400, 500 and 2000 moves, clear of the wall;\n5. the same ramps with the shank 1 mm into the wall (real findings);\n6. leaning rings whose pose changes at each move (the sweep c3 runs and finds hits);\n7. an empty path, and moves with NaN and infinite coordinates.\n\n## Counts (`ReachStats.sample_tests`; old = the reference, the code on main)\n\n| Path | Old | New |\n|---|---|---|\n| ramp 500 moves, shank 2 mm short of the wall | 2,004,000 | 0 |\n| ramp 2000 moves, the same | 32,016,000 | 0 |\n| ramp 500 moves, shank 1 mm into the wall | 1,394,117 | 20,192 |\n| ramp 2000 moves, the same | 22,197,103 | 322,697 |\n\nThe slices stay one for each distinct plane (500 and 2000). A disc near a wall still reads the wall samples of every plane: each plane's section has its own vertices on the wall, and an exact law must read them. The decision file has the reasons: `docs/decisions/3588-band-reach-sample-index.md`.\n\nThe tangency law (b) slices one time for each distinct Z also. The slice is its cost, so it needs its own design: #3593 (P3).\n\n## Gate (debug, final head)\n\n- Pins (sim lib, `verify::reach_tests` and `verify::sample_grid`): 5 of 5 pass, 0.71 s.\n  - `indexed_band_reach_keeps_every_finding_of_the_pooled_scan`\n  - `degenerate_paths_match_the_pooled_scan_and_do_not_panic`\n  - `a_many_plane_ramp_reads_only_the_samples_near_its_discs`\n  - `repeats_are_kept_once_and_every_near_sample_is_found`, `unplaceable_samples_and_queries_fall_back_to_all`\n- All `sim suites` steps (`cargo xtask gate batch --only \"sim suites\"`): 92 of 92 pass, 0 stalled, 3.6 min.\n- App binaries, one by one (`cargo test -p camcraft-app --test <name>`), all pass:\n  - `mcp_verify` 3/3; `strategy_fallback` 1/1; `offset_pass_start` 4/4 (2 ignored); `pocket_pass_start` 6/6 in 92.99 s (main: 89.6 s); `golden_cl` 33/33; `conformance` 7/7; `first_part_programs` 2/2; `example_catalog` 37/37 (4 ignored).\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-sim --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --lib -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- Light lane: dry run only. It has two motion twins, which this diff does not reach. Not run.\n- No golden, catalog program or pinned finding list moved.\n\n## Line counts\n\n`reach.rs` 287 -> 331. New: `sample_grid.rs` 140, `reach_reference.rs` 262 (test only), `reach_tests.rs` 169.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The release build timing.\n- PR #3577's fig8 case (`pocket_pass_start::fig8_pocket_outside_starts_each_level_at_the_stock_edge` on a trial merge with #3577).\n- The other app suites of the quick plan (124 steps), and the full heavy lanes (a P2 gets no heavy step).\n\nChangelog: The verifier no longer takes minutes on a toolpath with many cut planes (a ramp entry): the shank and holder reach check reads only the boundary samples near each move. The findings do not change.\n\nEffort: high\n\nRefs #3567\nRefs #3577\nRefs #3593\n\nFixes #3588\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
